 that the most  important component of the rosetta stone  staff software method  is the four squares screen  the user is presented with a page that  shows four pictures of various objects  or entities a pre-recorded phrase or  word is played back the user must click  on the square that contains a visual  answer blah blah blah  why does this work because it's very  instant feedback instant gratification  people are encouraged and they keep  studying  and of course the biggest thing in  anything is you have to stay with it  so rosetta stone tends to make people  want to continue studying if that's the  case that's great because obviously  people if they enjoy what they're doing  they're going to continue doing it  and i've said and as i have said many  times people are often discouraged in  traditional language classes  the reviewer goes on to say that in  normal language situations we don't get  positive feedback  there is a great deal of uncertainty  we perceive that we're doing poorly  we perceive that we're not  having success and therefore people quit  so you know that's perhaps not a fair  description of everything that happens  in rosetta stone but but based on that  description  and based on what i have heard from my  son mark  i have seven reasons why i am not very  interested in doing rosetta stone  uh i don't put there the eighth reason  which is because of my involvement with  link but the first thing is i don't like  sitting there and answering multiple  choice questions to me that is not a  natural i'm not communicating and as i  said in my book that i wrote some six  years ago  language learning should be about  communicating right from the beginning  if i'm listening to a real story i'm  communicating i'm communicating with the  language with the culture with some  situation so just identifying pictures  on a screen to me is not something that  i would like to do i don't think i would  do it very long  the second thing is that my main  language learning activity takes place  during what i call dead time  i mostly listen seventy percent of my  activity is listening listening while  jogging while doing the dishes while  driving while waiting in line i always  have my little ipod with me  so if i had to if my language learning  activity had to be committed time i.e  sitting in front of a computer screen  i don't think i would do very much i  just wouldn't find the time  the third thing is  that the idea to me that you can learn  these words and phrases here is red  green blue yellow  click on the right one there's a house a  car a horse  i  don't think that i learn words i mean i  i i think i forget them all  i learned them i forget them i learned  how i forget them i don't think i can  nail them down this week i'm going to  learn  house car horse  train then i'm gonna click through  tree flower green and yellow i don't  think i will retain them i know that i'm  learning words because the stuff that  i'm listening to  i'm understanding more and more of it  but at the same time i know i'm  forgetting words because very simple  words after a year i'll have forgotten  the most simple word so i know i learn  and forget  so i don't believe in learning things  that way nailing down things by you know  multiple choice questions  number four you know to the extent that  i learn words and phrases i find that i  have to learn them  as part of episodes stories larger  contexts  where i have tried like living language  and other systems like that where they  give you these phrases i can't remember  them  you can tell me at i was with some  people the other night some brazilians  they told me the name of this brazilian  drink they must have told me five times  what it was karambah or something i  don't know what it was i can't remember  it  but i can remember words in a larger  context where i remember that there was  a story and and these are stories that i  listen to and i even remember where i  was listening to it i was listening to  it while i was uh jogging in uh  somewhere whatever  so so i need the episode i need the  context  the next thing number five is in terms  of gratification  yeah getting something right on the  screen it might be fun for a while but  to me the big gratification  is when i start to be able to tell when  words begin and end  so that it's no longer just noise and  soon after that i actually start to make  sense of what i'm listening to initially  twenty percent thirty percent and sixty  percent and remember when i begin a  language i listen to stuff over and over  and over again  so that to me is the gratification  uh  identifying  house car  knowing that i'm going to forget it  anyway unless i see it in many many  different contexts meaningful context  that wouldn't be very gratifying to me  number six  you know my whole goal in learning  languages is to communicate  communication begins with understanding  understanding a real language and then  being able to express myself in real  situations and i know that that's a long  road i have the impression that rosetta  stone doesn't take me very far along  that road  so i'd be reluctant to get started and  the seventh thing second seventh and  final thing is  that even in terms of getting started  like some people say and i accept that  some people want to start with pimsleur  some people want to start with michelle  thomas  i have started languages with teach  yourself  with colloquial and these are all good  and they have the role and i would  prefer all of those as a method of  getting started because their language  their real language  i'm in a hurry to get into real language  to engage with the language real  situations  so  i don't so i want to listen to things  uh and read and improve you know work on  my vocabulary knowing that i'm going to  forget and learn forget and learn and  gradually the picture becomes clearer so  to me  a little bit of uncertainty at the  beginning  it doesn't bother me and the c